1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,075
Labuan: The Borneo Island
That Was Part of Singapore

2
00:00:04,075 --> 00:00:08,932
For almost forty years, a small
island off the coast of Borneo,

3
00:00:09,012 --> 00:00:12,380
about 1,100 kilometres
from Singapore across

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:16,300
the South China Sea, was
governed from Singapore.

5
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,942
From the 1st of January
1907 Labuan was part of the

6
00:00:20,022 --> 00:00:24,175
Settlement of Singapore, and
from 1912 it was a settlement

7
00:00:24,255 --> 00:00:27,241
of its own within the Straits Settlements,

8
00:00:27,321 --> 00:00:31,700
run by a Resident who answered
to the Governor in Singapore.

9
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:35,066
Today it is a Malaysian
federal territory and an

10
00:00:35,146 --> 00:00:38,224
offshore financial
centre, and few people in

11
00:00:38,304 --> 00:00:41,750
Singapore know that it
was once on the same map.

12
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:46,467
In the 1840s British steamers
sailing between Singapore and the new

13
00:00:46,547 --> 00:00:50,977
colony of Hong Kong needed coal
on the way, and Labuan had both

14
00:00:51,057 --> 00:00:55,925
coal seams and a deep, sheltered
harbour at the mouth of Brunei Bay.

15
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:59,273
Its name comes from
the Malay word labuhan,

16
00:00:59,353 --> 00:01:01,825
meaning a harbour or anchorage.

17
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:04,654
James Brooke, by then
the Rajah of Sarawak,

18
00:01:04,734 --> 00:01:07,575
pressed the British government to take it.

19
00:01:07,575 --> 00:01:11,298
On the 18th of December
1846 the Sultan of Brunei,

20
00:01:11,378 --> 00:01:16,242
Omar Ali Saifuddin the Second,
signed a treaty ceding the island,

21
00:01:16,322 --> 00:01:20,958
and on the 24th of December the
British flag was hoisted there

22
00:01:21,038 --> 00:01:25,450
in the presence of Captain
Rodney Mundy of the Royal Navy.

23
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:29,954
Labuan became a Crown Colony
and a free port in 1848,

24
00:01:30,034 --> 00:01:32,975
with Brooke as its first Governor.

25
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:36,512
His Lieutenant-Governor
was William Napier,

26
00:01:36,592 --> 00:01:40,969
a Singapore lawyer who had
helped found the Singapore

27
00:01:41,049 --> 00:01:44,750
Free Press in 1835 and
edited it until 1846.

28
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:48,228
Napier's secretary on
the island was Hugh Low,

29
00:01:48,308 --> 00:01:51,708
later known as the
British Resident of Perak,

30
00:01:51,788 --> 00:01:55,500
who married Napier's
daughter Catherine in 1848.

31
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:56,982
That same year Napier,

32
00:01:57,062 --> 00:02:01,880
as Lieutenant-Governor of Labuan,
invested Brooke with his knighthood

33
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:05,225
at a ceremony in the
Singapore Assembly Rooms.

34
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:08,852
Some people in Singapore
worried that the new colony

35
00:02:08,932 --> 00:02:11,561
would draw trade away from their port,

36
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:15,196
and Singapore's centenary
history, written in 1921,

37
00:02:15,276 --> 00:02:19,625
remembered the gloomy predictions
made when Labuan was ceded.

38
00:02:19,625 --> 00:02:21,775
They came to nothing.

39
00:02:21,775 --> 00:02:26,423
Several British companies, starting
with the Eastern Archipelago

40
00:02:26,503 --> 00:02:30,412
Company in 1849, tried to
mine coal at Tanjung Kubong,

41
00:02:30,492 --> 00:02:34,850
at the northern tip of the
island, but the coal never paid.

42
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:38,833
Malaria, accidents and poor
management all took their toll,

43
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:41,875
and the ships went on
calling at Singapore.

44
00:02:41,875 --> 00:02:45,706
Brooke himself had to answer
a commission of inquiry,

45
00:02:45,786 --> 00:02:52,052
held in Singapore in 1854, into his campaigns
against the pirates of the Borneo coast,

46
00:02:52,132 --> 00:02:55,600
and it cleared him of
the most serious charges.

47
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,494
The best-known relic of the
mining years is the Chimney,

48
00:02:59,574 --> 00:03:03,255
a brick tower about 32 metres
tall at Tanjung Kubong,

49
00:03:03,335 --> 00:03:07,025
although nobody is quite
sure what it was built for.

50
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:11,325
Singapore and Labuan also
shared a church for a time.

51
00:03:11,325 --> 00:03:15,856
From the late 1860s they were
in the same Anglican diocese,

52
00:03:15,936 --> 00:03:20,312
the Diocese of Singapore,
Labuan and Sarawak, and in 1870

53
00:03:20,392 --> 00:03:24,925
Saint Andrew's Church in
Singapore was made its cathedral.

54
00:03:24,925 --> 00:03:27,871
The diocese was only divided in 1909,

55
00:03:27,951 --> 00:03:33,675
when Singapore got a bishop of its
own and Labuan and Sarawak another.

56
00:03:33,675 --> 00:03:38,770
By the 1880s the colony was deep in
debt, and from the 1st of January

57
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:43,870
1890 the British government handed
its administration to the British

58
00:03:43,950 --> 00:03:48,675
North Borneo Company, which
governed it alongside North Borneo.

59
00:03:48,675 --> 00:03:52,949
In 1894 a submarine
telegraph cable linked Labuan

60
00:03:53,029 --> 00:03:56,850
with North Borneo,
Singapore and Hong Kong.

61
00:03:56,850 --> 00:04:01,945
One of the governors the company
sent there, from 1900 to 1901,

62
00:04:02,025 --> 00:04:06,625
was Hugh Clifford, a young
colonial officer from Malaya.

63
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:10,926
Company rule did not solve the
island's problems, and in 1906

64
00:04:11,006 --> 00:04:15,100
Britain took it back and looked
for a better arrangement.

65
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:19,787
Letters Patent of the 30th of
October 1906 made Labuan part

66
00:04:19,867 --> 00:04:24,311
of the Colony of the Straits
Settlements from a date the

67
00:04:24,391 --> 00:04:29,400
Governor would proclaim, and he
chose the 1st of January 1907.

68
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:34,125
From that day the island was part
of the Settlement of Singapore.

69
00:04:34,125 --> 00:04:38,220
The next afternoon the Legislative
Council in Singapore passed

70
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,530
the Labuan Bill, which provided
for the island's administration,

71
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:45,426
through all its stages
in a single sitting,

72
00:04:45,506 --> 00:04:49,950
and the Straits Times reported it
the same day in a one-line note.

73
00:04:49,950 --> 00:04:54,946
On the 1st of December 1912 Labuan
was made a separate settlement,

74
00:04:55,026 --> 00:05:00,025
the fourth in the colony alongside
Singapore, Penang and Malacca.

75
00:05:00,025 --> 00:05:02,449
It stayed part of the same colony,

76
00:05:02,529 --> 00:05:06,800
and the Governor in Singapore
was also Governor of Labuan.

77
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,589
Between 1907 and the war
that meant Sir John Anderson,

78
00:05:10,669 --> 00:05:14,960
Sir Arthur Young, Sir Laurence
Guillemard, Sir Hugh Clifford,

79
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:18,050
Sir Cecil Clementi and Sir Shenton Thomas.

80
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:22,198
On the island itself the
government was run by a Resident,

81
00:05:22,278 --> 00:05:25,552
who, unlike the Resident
Councillor of Penang,

82
00:05:25,632 --> 00:05:28,475
had no seat on the Legislative Council.

83
00:05:28,475 --> 00:05:33,000
Hugh Clifford came back to Labuan's
affairs from the other end.

84
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,667
Nearly thirty years after
he had governed the island

85
00:05:36,747 --> 00:05:40,413
for the North Borneo Company,
he was Governor of the

86
00:05:40,493 --> 00:05:44,088
Straits Settlements in
Singapore from 1927 to 1930,

87
00:05:44,168 --> 00:05:48,275
and Labuan was one of the four
settlements in his charge.

88
00:05:48,275 --> 00:05:51,250
Labuan was a quiet posting.

89
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:56,087
The coal mines closed for good
in 1911, after years of losses,

90
00:05:56,167 --> 00:06:00,925
and the island made the news
in Singapore only now and then.

91
00:06:00,925 --> 00:06:04,604
In May 1916 the Resident, E. B. Maundrell,

92
00:06:04,684 --> 00:06:09,078
was shot dead by Sikh
policemen, as the chronology

93
00:06:09,158 --> 00:06:13,275
in Singapore's 1921
centenary history records.

94
00:06:13,275 --> 00:06:17,320
On the 17th of May 1922 the
Prince of Wales landed on

95
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:21,912
Labuan from the battlecruiser
HMS Renown, seven weeks after

96
00:06:21,992 --> 00:06:26,737
he had laid the foundation stone
of the Cenotaph in Singapore,

97
00:06:26,817 --> 00:06:30,475
and went on from there
to Jesselton and Brunei.

98
00:06:30,475 --> 00:06:34,995
Its maps were made in Singapore
too, and the map of the island

99
00:06:35,075 --> 00:06:39,750
shown here was produced by the
Survey Department there in 1930.

100
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:44,650
By the last pre-war count, in
1941, about 9,000 people lived

101
00:06:44,730 --> 00:06:49,875
on the island, whose one town,
Victoria, stood on the harbour.

102
00:06:49,875 --> 00:06:54,226
Japanese forces landed on
Labuan in the first days

103
00:06:54,306 --> 00:06:58,825
of January 1942, a few
weeks before Singapore fell.

104
00:06:58,825 --> 00:07:01,650
Its airfield mattered to them.

105
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:06,321
Lieutenant-General Toshinari
Maeda, who commanded the Japanese

106
00:07:06,401 --> 00:07:11,608
forces in Borneo, was killed in an
air crash at Bintulu on the 5th of

107
00:07:11,688 --> 00:07:16,283
September 1942 while on his way
to open it, and on the 9th of

108
00:07:16,363 --> 00:07:21,650
December 1942 the Japanese renamed
Labuan Maeda Island in his memory.

109
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:26,973
On the 10th of June 1945 Australian
troops of the 24th Brigade,

110
00:07:27,053 --> 00:07:31,089
9th Australian Division,
landed on the island in

111
00:07:31,169 --> 00:07:35,800
Operation Oboe Six, the first
step in retaking Borneo.

112
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:39,775
The fighting went on
until the 21st of June.

113
00:07:39,775 --> 00:07:44,097
Thirty-four Australians and
389 Japanese were killed,

114
00:07:44,177 --> 00:07:47,418
and the Allied bombing and shelling left

115
00:07:47,498 --> 00:07:51,900
Victoria in ruins and about
3,000 civilians homeless.

116
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:56,682
On the 10th of September 1945
Lieutenant-General Masao Baba,

117
00:07:56,762 --> 00:08:01,220
commander of the Japanese
37th Army, surrendered all the

118
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:06,083
Japanese forces in Borneo to
Major-General George Wootten of

119
00:08:06,163 --> 00:08:11,025
the 9th Division at Layang-Layangan,
on Labuan's west coast.

120
00:08:11,025 --> 00:08:14,550
The place is now called Surrender Point.

121
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:19,280
Two days later, on the 12th of
September, the Japanese commanders in

122
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:24,100
Southeast Asia surrendered in
Singapore, at the Municipal Building.

123
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:28,600
Labuan also holds a large
Commonwealth war cemetery.

124
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:31,958
The Labuan War Cemetery has 3,908 graves,

125
00:08:32,038 --> 00:08:36,402
and most of the men buried
there died as prisoners of

126
00:08:36,482 --> 00:08:40,675
war in the Japanese camps
at Sandakan and Kuching.

127
00:08:40,675 --> 00:08:43,950
Many had been sent there from Singapore.

128
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:48,824
After the fall of Singapore,
the Japanese shipped two groups

129
00:08:48,904 --> 00:08:52,787
of Australian prisoners
from Changi to Sandakan,

130
00:08:52,867 --> 00:08:58,402
about 1,500 in "B" Force in 1942
and about 500 in "E" Force in 1943,

131
00:08:58,482 --> 00:09:02,775
to build an airfield, and
very few of them survived.

132
00:09:02,775 --> 00:09:06,776
Their graves were moved to
Labuan after the war because the

133
00:09:06,856 --> 00:09:11,687
Sandakan site flooded, and because
the camp records had been destroyed,

134
00:09:11,767 --> 00:09:15,225
more than half of the
Sandakan graves are unnamed.

135
00:09:15,225 --> 00:09:19,595
Baba was later tried for
the Sandakan death marches

136
00:09:19,675 --> 00:09:24,125
and was hanged at Rabaul
on the 7th of August 1947.

137
00:09:24,125 --> 00:09:28,735
The Straits Settlements were
dissolved on the 1st of April 1946,

138
00:09:28,815 --> 00:09:32,179
and Singapore became a
Crown Colony on its own,

139
00:09:32,259 --> 00:09:36,875
with Labuan still under its
jurisdiction for a few more months.

140
00:09:36,875 --> 00:09:41,816
On the 15th of July 1946 the
island was joined to the new Crown

141
00:09:41,896 --> 00:09:47,475
Colony of North Borneo, which took
over from the North Borneo Company.

142
00:09:47,475 --> 00:09:51,509
The next morning's Straits Times
reported on its front page that,

143
00:09:51,589 --> 00:09:54,611
"with Labuan transferred
from the jurisdiction of

144
00:09:54,691 --> 00:09:58,789
Singapore," North Borneo had become
a British colony at a ceremony

145
00:09:58,869 --> 00:10:02,460
in the bullet-scarred Survey
Hall in Jesselton, one of the

146
00:10:02,540 --> 00:10:06,275
few government buildings there
that the bombing had spared.

147
00:10:06,275 --> 00:10:09,520
In 1963 North Borneo, renamed Sabah,

148
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:14,125
became part of Malaysia,
and Labuan went with it.

149
00:10:14,125 --> 00:10:20,039
On the 16th of April 1984 Sabah handed
the island to the federal government,

150
00:10:20,119 --> 00:10:22,800
and it became a federal territory.

151
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:26,887
On the 1st of October 1990
Malaysia declared Labuan an

152
00:10:26,967 --> 00:10:30,976
international offshore
financial centre, now known as

153
00:10:31,056 --> 00:10:35,300
the Labuan International
Business and Financial Centre.

154
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:40,889
At the end of 2024 it had 838
licensed financial institutions,

155
00:10:40,969 --> 00:10:45,551
627 new Labuan companies
had been set up that year,

156
00:10:45,631 --> 00:10:51,300
and its licensed financial sector
employed about 5,000 people.

157
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:53,611
The island is still a free port,

158
00:10:53,691 --> 00:10:57,950
and ferries run to it from
Kota Kinabalu and from Brunei.

159
00:10:57,950 --> 00:11:01,402
Labuan's land area is
about 92 square kilometres,

160
00:11:01,482 --> 00:11:06,951
roughly an eighth of Singapore's. Its
history is kept in a handful of places:

161
00:11:07,031 --> 00:11:10,555
the Chimney, now a small
museum, the war cemetery,

162
00:11:10,635 --> 00:11:15,240
the memorial at Surrender Point,
and a row of old memorial stones

163
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:19,276
outside the Labuan Museum
that mark the cession of 1846,

164
00:11:19,356 --> 00:11:23,825
the Prince of Wales's visit of
1922 and General Maeda's death.

165
00:11:23,825 --> 00:11:28,000
In Singapore the link
has left almost no trace.

166
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,265
Saint Andrew's Cathedral
was once the seat of a

167
00:11:31,345 --> 00:11:35,108
bishop whose diocese reached
Labuan, and the island is

168
00:11:35,188 --> 00:11:40,231
listed in old atlases and directories
as one of the Straits Settlements,

169
00:11:40,311 --> 00:11:42,375
alongside Penang and Malacca.

170
00:11:42,375 --> 00:11:44,618
Where it fits in the bigger story:

171
00:11:44,698 --> 00:11:48,525
Singapore's borders were not
always the ones it has now.

172
00:11:48,525 --> 00:11:51,886
Like Christmas Island
and the Cocos Islands,

173
00:11:51,966 --> 00:11:56,657
which are told in the Christmas
Island post, Labuan was for a

174
00:11:56,738 --> 00:12:01,037
time run from Singapore as
part of one scattered colony,

175
00:12:01,118 --> 00:12:04,950
from 1907 until the colony
was broken up in 1946.

176
00:12:04,950 --> 00:12:08,850
The island never became the
rival port that some people in

177
00:12:08,930 --> 00:12:13,448
Singapore feared in the 1840s, but
for almost forty years it shared

178
00:12:13,528 --> 00:12:17,497
Singapore's governors and laws,
for about forty years until

179
00:12:17,577 --> 00:12:21,546
1909 it shared its bishop, and
its war cemetery still holds

180
00:12:21,626 --> 00:12:25,950
many of the men who left Changi
for Borneo and never came back.
